#   Beagle-lite

This project implements beagle-lite functionality for git repos.

Usage:

 -  `lite [--plain] <path>...` — file/dir args page interactively at a terminal, dump as plain hunks when piped or under `--plain`.
 -  `lite index [<repo>]` — index a git repo (root, `.git` dir or gitfile; default the cwd) into its own `<repo>/.git/be/` and list it in `$HOME/.config/be/tracks`. Lazy: a second run is a watermark no-op, a later one indexes only the gap. Everything under `.git/be/` is derived — `rm -rf` it and the next run rebuilds it from the ODB.
 -  `lite log [--plain] [<hex>|<path>]` — the commit log off that index (bare = the checked-out tip, `<hex>` = a commit's ancestors, anything else = a file's revisions), one `<sha8> <date> <subject> (<author>)` row per commit, newest first. At a terminal it pages as a coloured hunk, the same way a file arg does; piped or under `--plain` it is the bare rows. It brings the index up to date itself, so there is never an `index` call to remember.
 -  `lite commit [--plain] [<hex>]` — one commit's metadata: the header block in the object's own order (folded `gpgsig`/`mergetag` continuations and `encoding` included), then the message. Bare = the checked-out tip, otherwise any 6..40 hexlet. The plain bytes are exactly `commit <sha40>` + `git cat-file commit <sha>`, so a pipe gets the object itself; at a terminal the same bytes page as a coloured hunk. Pure ODB — it reads no index, so it answers on a repo that was never indexed and on a commit off the branch.
 -  `lite diff [--plain] [<hex>|<path>]` — the change, as be's CFOLD 2-layer diff shows it: bare = the worktree against HEAD over the tracked paths, `<path>` = that path only (a file whole, a dir scoped), `<hex>` = that commit against its FIRST parent. At a terminal each changed file is a hunk in the pager, added tokens washed salad green and removed ones salmon; piped or under `--plain` it is the unified `--- / +++ / @@` bytes under the usual `hunk <uri>` banner. It reads the ODB and the worktree only — no index, so it works in a repo whose `.git/be` was never built. It does not read `.git/index` (a STAGED-only add is invisible), detects no renames, recurses no submodule and applies no gitattributes filter, so a CRLF-checkout repo reports the eol difference git normalizes away.
 -  `lite install [<repo>]` — make lite git's merge driver for that repo (root, `.git` dir or gitfile; default the cwd): the driver command goes into `.git/config` as `merge.lite.driver` (git never reads driver commands from a tracked file) and `* merge=lite` into `.git/info/attributes`, so the install is purely local and uncommitted. A second run changes nothing.
 -  `lite merge <base> <ours> <theirs> [-o <out>] [-p <path>]` — the driver itself, and a usable three-file merge on its own. It CRDT-weaves the three blobs (base folds first, ours and theirs fold concurrently on it), writes the merged bytes over `<ours>` (or `<out>`) and exits 0 when they are clean, 1 when they are not. Edits that only git's line-at-a-time merge calls a conflict — two words of the same line, adjacent lines — merge cleanly here. A real conflict is written MARKERLESS: both sides' tokens in weave order, no `<<<<`/`====`/`>>>>` fences, so the file stays tokenizable and a later merge weaves over it (`git mergetool` finds no fences by design, DIS-080). `-p` names the real path, which is where the tokenizer's language comes from — the driver's `%O %A %B` temps have junk names. A binary or over-4-MB file is not weavable: it goes to `git merge-file`, never to a silent ours.

 -  `lite chat [dir] [outdir]` — render the Claude Code session logs of a project dir (both args default `.`) as StrictMark pages: every `*.jsonl` under `~/.claude/projects/<mangled abs path>/` becomes `<outdir>/<basename>.mkd`, strict 1:1. A page is a plain conversation — a `#   Session: <date> <time> <user>` head, user turns blockquoted, Claude's prose verbatim, one 4-backtick fence per assistant row holding one line per tool call. Tool results, thinking, sidechains and every machine-injected XML envelope are dropped. The last line is the hidden ref def `[log]: file:/<abs jsonl> "<bytes>"`, so a rerun is append-only off that cursor (grown log → only the new turns, rewritten log → a fresh page, unchanged log → not even a write). It reports one `chat: wrote <path>` per page written and stays silent when everything is up to date; `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` overrides the Claude home.

Build: quickjab is standalone — it carries dog/ (libdog) and test/ (the
jab-shared API suite) as submodules, no jab tree involved:

    cmake -S <quickjab> -B build -GNinja \
        -DJAB_JSRC=<this tree> -DQUICKJAB_JSRC_PACK=ON
    ninja -C build quickjab

